• Endogenous Linear Program (LP)-Based Allocation vs. Exogenous Heuristics: Traditional allocation methods operate as exogenous ex-post accounting exercises after physical flows are fixed. In contrast, an endogenous approach formulates scenario-wise allocation fractions (
α i S
) as decision variables within a dedicated LP operating over fixed Pareto-optimal physical flows

The proposed two-stage hierarchical architecture circumvents this difficulty through principled decoupling. Level 1, formulated as a tri-objective stochastic MILP, optimizes all physical flow and network design decisions while remaining agnostic to the specific allocation fractions. Level 2, formulated as a pure LP, subsequently determines the gain-sharing allocations (
α i s
) by treating Level 1’s optimal cost outcomes (
C S
total
) as fixed parameters. This sequential structure establishes a strictly unidirectional information flow without an iterative feedback loop from Level 2 back to Level 1. In this setting, the gain-sharing mechanism is “endogenous” because the scenario-wise allocation fractions (
α i s
) are decision variables optimized within the Level 2 LP, rather than being assigned via exogenous post-hoc accounting rules; however, this allocation is computed over fixed physical flow solutions generated at Level 1.

• Gap 1 (Exogenous Post-Hoc Rules vs. Endogenous LP-Based Gain-Sharing): While cost/profit allocation mechanisms such as the Shapley value, nucleolus, and EPML are well-established in collaborative logistics [36], [37], [38], they are predominantly applied as exogenous post-hoc accounting rules after physical flows are fixed. In contrast, the proposed EPML engine endogenizes allocation fractions (
α i s
) as decision variables within a dedicated LP operating over fixed Pareto-optimal physical flow decisions. This ensures that profit distribution is optimized to maximize relative equity (
g
min
) rather than imposed via rigid external accounting heuristics, while avoiding non-convex integrated formulations.

